## .NET 8 Migration: `TimedExecutor`

### Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 6+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also removed** — no abort/reset pattern exists
3. The idiomatic replacement is `CancellationToken` + `Task` with cooperative cancellation

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Async overload (preferred)
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Non-cooperative work** | Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, cancellation is *cooperative* — the `work` delegate must check `ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` or pass the token to I/O APIs. Uncooperative code will **not** stop. |
| **Signature change** | Callers must update from `Func<T>` to `Func<CancellationToken, T>`. This is a breaking API change. |
| **`task.Wait()` vs `GetAwaiter().GetResult()`** | `Wait()` wraps in `AggregateException`; `GetAwaiter().GetResult()` preserves the original exception. Pick based on your error-handling contract. |
| **Fire-and-forget leaks** | If you cancel but the work ignores the token, the `Task` keeps running silently, consuming resources. Audit all call sites. |
| **Thread pool starvation** | `Task.Run` uses the thread pool. Long-running CPU work should use `Task.Factory.StartNew(..., TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning)`. |

### Wrapping Uncooperative Code (Escape Hatch)

If you **cannot** modify the work to accept a `CancellationToken` (e.g., third-party blocking call):

```csharp
// ⚠️ Last resort — the background thread leaks if it doesn't finish
public T ExecuteWithTimeoutUncooperative<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    T result = default!;
    Exception? error = null;

    var thread = new Thread(() =>
    {
        try { result = work(); }
        catch (Exception ex) { error = ex; }
    }) { IsBackground = true }; // won't block process exit

    thread.Start();

    if (!thread.Join(timeout))
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout (thread still running)");

    if (error != null)
        throw new AggregateException(error);

    return result;
}
```

> **This leaks the thread** — there is no safe equivalent to `Thread.Abort()` in modern .NET. The cooperative cancellation pattern is the only correct solution.
